  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM EDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nToday-Tonight...Ridging aloft will keep the surface high across \nthe Atlantic in place, with the surface ridge axis extending \ntowards the Florida peninsula. This will result in persistent \nlight and variable winds locally through this morning until the \neast coast sea breeze develops and pushes inland, with winds \nbecoming south to southeast around 10 mph through this afternoon. \nIsolated to scattered shower and storm development will be \npossible in association with the sea breeze and its intrusion \ninland, but chances for development remain low (20-40%). The best \nchances (30-40%) are focused across the Treasure Coast and near \nLake Okeechobee where a plume of moisture lifts northward today. \nNorth of this area, a slightly drier air mass will limit rain and \nstorm chances to around 20%. Sufficient daytime heating across the\narea today will provide enough energy for convective development \nand drier air aloft will act to enhance any potential wind gusts. \nAny storms that develop will be capable of producing lightning \nstrikes, wind gusts up to 50 mph, and brief heavy downpours. \nActivity is anticipated to diminish late this afternoon into the \nevening hours with a return of dry conditions overnight. \n\nThe persistent high pressure both at the surface and aloft will \nsupport a continued warming trend across east central Florida \ntoday. Afternoon temperatures are forecast to reach the low to mid\n90s across much of east central Florida, with some locations near\nand north of the I-4 corridor climbing into the upper 90s and\nnearing record temperatures. These temperatures combined with \nhumid conditions will produce peak heat indices of 108F to 112F \ntoday, and a Heat Advisory has been issued between 10 AM and 8 PM \nacross all of east central Florida. Those spending extended \nperiods of time outdoors are strongly encouraged to remain \nadequately hydrated, wear lightweight, lightly-colored clothing, \nand take frequent breaks in the shade or an air conditioned \nbuilding to help reduce the risk of developing heat-related \nillness. Muggy conditions will continue into the overnight hours, \nwith lows in the mid to upper 70s tonight.\n\nSunday...Ridging aloft and at the surface will remain in place \nacross east central Florida on Sunday, with a plume of Saharan \ndust anticipated to drift across the peninsula. This will result \nin even lower rain and storm chances across the area (20%). Any \nstorms that do manage to develop may be capable of producing \nlightning strikes, wind gusts to 50 mph, and brief downpours. \nActivity will diminish into the evening and overnight hours. The \ncombination of the dry air aloft and the persistent high will \nsupport a continued warming pattern locally, with afternoon highs \nreaching the mid to upper 90s areawide. Maximum apparent \ntemperatures are anticipated to once again reach and/or exceed \n108F areawide, and a Heat Advisory will likely be needed once \nagain across east central Florida. Practicing heat safety will \ncontinue to be strongly encouraged through the remainder of this \nweekend in order to reduce the risk of heat illness. Little relief\nis anticipated into the overnight hours with lows remaining above\nnormal in the mid to upper 70s.\n\nMonday-Friday...Ridging aloft becomes broader into next week with\nthe Atlantic high sliding southward as a weakening frontal \nboundary drifts southward across the southeastern US towards the \nFlorida peninsula. Moisture is forecast to increase locally, with \na return to near-normal rain and storm chances across northern \nportions of the area on Monday and areawide Tuesday onward. \nConvection will continue to follow a diurnal pattern, with peak \ncoverage happening during the afternoon and early evening hours in\nassociation with the sea breezes. Lightning, gusty winds, and \nheavy downpours will all be possible with activity. Showers and \nstorms are anticipated to diminish into the late evening and \novernight hours. Aside from shower and storm chances, heat will \ncontinue to be a concern through next week. Afternoon highs are \nforecast to remain above normal areawide with humid conditions \nstill leading to peak heat indices near or even above Heat \nAdvisory criteria.",
